WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:22.000
Price return. The price difference is the sale price minus the purchase price. A higher sale price alone does not tell the full story.

00:00:22.000 --> 00:00:44.000
An example purchase. Imagine buying 10 shares for $20 each. The purchase costs $200 before any trading fees.

00:00:44.000 --> 00:01:06.000
An example sale. If the shares are sold for $23 each, the sale proceeds are $230. The price gain before costs is $30.

00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:28.000
Include costs. Suppose total purchase and sale costs are $4. The example gain after those costs is $26, before taxes.

00:01:28.000 --> 00:01:50.000
Consider a dividend. A company may pay a dividend during ownership. A $0.50 dividend per share on 10 shares would add $5 before taxes.

00:01:50.000 --> 00:02:12.000
A loss scenario. If the sale price is $17 per share, proceeds are $170. The price loss is $30 before costs or dividends.

00:02:12.000 --> 00:02:34.000
No guaranteed payout. A company can reduce or stop dividends. Stock prices also move in either direction; a previous gain predicts nothing.

00:02:34.000 --> 00:02:56.000
Check the full picture. Record purchase price, sale price, number of shares, costs, and any distributions before describing a return.
